<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="no"?>
<!--
    Body condition: an individual nutrition budget and body reserve for cattle (scripts/nutrition).
    Beef animals show a finish score (0..100), cows a BCS (1..5); both come from one reserve r
    (0..1). The reserve is part of the live weight: one unit of r is reserveCapacityRatio x the
    animal's target weight, fixed when its condition is first set up. Health and the health gene
    keep their own meaning.

    Covered: cattle (animal type COW) from calfLegacyUntilAgeMonth on, in a pen with a feed trough.
    Calves, other species and Hof Bergmann's MILKCOW keep the old rules. Under the weight model v2
    (weightProfiles.xml) covered cattle - and MILKCOW where the game holds it as individual animals,
    on the Holstein female curve - take the same budget from birth with their own frame, reserve
    mass and development (scripts/animal/RLGrowth.lua); the yield, price and conception factors
    still start at calfLegacyUntilAgeMonth. The reserve index r is then their reserve mass over
    reserveCapacityRatio of their frame's live weight at the reference reserve.

    Every hour the pen's actual consumption (trough and meadow) is shared between the animals by
    their food request. The ration supports a share of each animal's full-feed demand: liters
    eaten x energy of each fill type x the breed's grass/hay/silage trait (breedTraits.xml), over
    the liters requested. The budget pays maintenance, pregnancy, then growth and milk; a shortfall
    is drawn from the reserve, a surplus stored in it. An average animal on a full TMR ration keeps
    the old growth line and milk yield.

    enabled                    Off: the old weight and milk rules; saved conditions are kept.
    calfLegacyUntilAgeMonth    Younger cattle keep the old rules.
    fixedFeedPrice             How much a liter eaten is worth (server only).
                               false: what this animal needs on full feed - maintenance at its
                               own weight, its own growth (a steer's x1.15, a sick animal's less)
                               and milk - so needing more makes the same liters richer.
                               true: what an animal of its genes on its full-feed growth line at
                               its age and state needs, intact and healthy, whatever this one
                               needs now; a disease's lost growth is spent on the disease. Animals
                               behind their line (slow forage routes) get only maintenance-level
                               feed once the line is grown, so this stays off; see
                               tests/test_weight.lua. Under the weight model v2 the reference is an
                               average animal on its breed's curve at this one's age; it still
                               slows hay (an Angus bull 35.8 months to slaughter instead of 24.3)
                               and stalls a water buffalo bull on grass near 350 kg, so the forage
                               energies and intakes need calibrating for it first.

    Reserve
    initialReserve             Reserve of a cow or heifer whose condition is first set up
                               (an old savegame, a calf leaving the legacy rules, a dealer animal).
                               Bulls and steers start on their finishing curve instead.
    reserveCapacityRatio       Live weight of one unit of reserve, as a share of target weight.
    referenceReserve           Reserve at which live weight equals frame weight.
    minReserve                 Floor the reserve is never drawn below.
    gainRatePerMonth           Most the reserve rises in a month (0.10 = 0.4 BCS).
    lossRatePerMonth           Most the reserve falls in a month.
    mobilizeEaseRange          Within this distance of the floor the reserve gives up less.
    trendTauMonths             Time constant of the condition trend shown to the player.

    Energy (NU, a relative unit, per period)
    maintenancePerKgMetabolic  Maintenance per kg^0.75 of live weight.
    growthNUPerKg              A kg of frame growth.
    depositNUPerKg             A kg of reserve laid down. Must exceed mobilizeNUPerKg.
    mobilizeNUPerKg            What a kg of reserve gives back.
    catabolismNUPerKg          What a kg of frame gives back once the reserve is spent.
    maxCatabolismPerMonth      Most of its weight an animal loses from frame in a month.
    frameExcessTauMonths       Frame above the target weight returns to it at this time constant.
    milkNUPerLiter             A liter of milk as the game counts it (per-period curve).
    pregnancyFactor            Extra demand per fetus at term, as a share of maintenance,
    pregnancyRampStart         rising from this share of the pregnancy on.

    Intake: budget = ration share x capacity x full-feed demand (root defaults; per animalType,
    breed or subType below)
    intakeGrowing              Growing animals and bulls (the old growthNutritionFactor).
    intakeLactating            Lactating cows. Below 1 a full ration cannot cover peak lactation.
    intakeDry                  Dry cows that have calved. Above 1 they fatten on a rich ration.
    lactationMeanFactor        The lactation curve's mean: a cow's demand at full feed counts
                               its milk at this point of the curve.
    highYieldIntake            A lactating cow eats 1 + this x (productivity - 1) of her request,
    maxIntakeFactor            at most this much.
    milkPriority               Weight of milk against growth when the budget falls short.
    milkMobilizeShare          Share of the milk a cow may fund from her reserve,
    milkMobilizeFloor          and none once her reserve is below this (BCS 2.2): a thin cow gives
                               less milk instead.

    Ceilings
    heiferReserveCeiling       A heifer stores a surplus up to this reserve,
    cowReserveCeiling          a cow that has calved up to this one.

    Curves (points are x="..." y="..."; linear between, flat outside)
    finishingTarget  x = weight / slaughter reference weight (carcassYield.xml), y = the reserve a
                     bull or steer on full feed lays down at that weight. Steers read it at
                     x + steerFinishShift.
    finishScore      x = reserve, y = finish score 0..100.
    yield            x = finish score, y = carcass yield factor (meat kg, slaughter value floor).
    market           x = finish score, y = factor on the weight part of a live price.
    fertility        x = BCS, y = factor on a cow's conception probability.
    lossTrend        x = reserve loss per month (trend), y = further factor on conception.
    feedEnergy       Energy of a liter of each fill type. Unlisted food fill types use the
                     productionWeight of their food group (animalFood.xml). Hay, grass and
                     silage sit above their productionWeight so that a full growth line on TMR
                     stays exact while the forage routes stay close to the old ones (silage
                     +0-1 month to finish, hay +1-1.5, grass about the same, Highland on grass
                     faster) and a dry cow keeps her condition on hay. See
                     tests/test_bodycondition.lua.

    species: a section for one animal type (sheep): the root with the attributes and curves it
    sets replaced, plus femaleFinishShift (a young female reads finishingTarget at x + this),
    youngUntilMonth (from this continuous age an animal that never had young is grown: no fat
    class, and a female leaves the finishing target - and femaleFinishShift - for
    heiferReserveCeiling, the section's maiden-female ceiling), fertilityEffect (0: condition
    leaves conception as it is) and fatClass elements (the class a reserve shows: the last whose
    minReserve it reaches). Goats and every other type read the root.

    To change these without editing the mod, copy this file to
    modSettings/FS25_RealisticLivestockRM/bodyCondition.xml; that copy then replaces this file
    entirely. An invalid file switches the feature off and logs why.
-->
<bodyCondition enabled="true" calfLegacyUntilAgeMonth="6" fixedFeedPrice="false"
    initialReserve="0.45" reserveCapacityRatio="0.20" referenceReserve="0.45" minReserve="0.05"
    gainRatePerMonth="0.07" lossRatePerMonth="0.08" mobilizeEaseRange="0.15" trendTauMonths="0.5"
    maintenancePerKgMetabolic="4.5" growthNUPerKg="40" depositNUPerKg="48" mobilizeNUPerKg="32"
    catabolismNUPerKg="10" maxCatabolismPerMonth="0.05" frameExcessTauMonths="1"
    milkNUPerLiter="11" pregnancyFactor="0.35" pregnancyRampStart="0.6"
    intakeGrowing="1.10" intakeLactating="1.02" intakeDry="1.6" lactationMeanFactor="1.08"
    highYieldIntake="0.8" maxIntakeFactor="1.25" milkPriority="2" milkMobilizeShare="0.15"
    milkMobilizeFloor="0.30" heiferReserveCeiling="0.55" cowReserveCeiling="0.75" steerFinishShift="0.08">

    <!--
        Intake by breed (intakeGrowing, intakeLactating and intakeDry may be set on an animalType,
        breed or subType element).
        Dairy: TMR counts as silage for the breed traits, so a Holstein (silage 1.10) gets 10% more
        from it than a Swiss Brown; her lactating intake is set so both breeds run the same cycle on
        TMR with the dry period on hay: about BCS 3.1-3.4 at calving, 2.5-2.8 at the bottom, 98% of
        the milk.
        Suckler breeds eat much more forage than their small milk needs; with the dairy intake a
        grazing Angus herd would sit at BCS 1.6-1.8. At these values a suckler herd keeps BCS
        2.2-2.7 on grass (Highland, Hereford best) and 2.3-2.7 or more on hay, and gets fat (BCS 4)
        on TMR.
    -->
    <animalType name="COW">
        <breed name="HOLSTEIN" intakeLactating="0.93"/>
        <breed name="ANGUS"    intakeLactating="1.3" intakeDry="1.8"/>
        <breed name="LIMOUSIN" intakeLactating="1.3" intakeDry="1.8"/>
        <breed name="HEREFORD" intakeLactating="1.3" intakeDry="1.8"/>
        <breed name="HIGHLAND" intakeLactating="1.3" intakeDry="1.8"/>
    </animalType>

    <finishingTarget>
        <point x="0.50" y="0.35"/>
        <point x="0.80" y="0.42"/>
        <point x="1.00" y="0.52"/>
        <point x="1.15" y="0.65"/>
        <point x="1.30" y="0.78"/>
        <point x="1.50" y="0.90"/>
    </finishingTarget>

    <finishScore>
        <point x="0.00" y="0"/>
        <point x="0.20" y="25"/>
        <point x="0.35" y="50"/>
        <point x="0.45" y="65"/>
        <point x="0.60" y="80"/>
        <point x="0.75" y="90"/>
        <point x="1.00" y="100"/>
    </finishScore>

    <yield>
        <point x="0" y="0.92"/>
        <point x="25" y="0.95"/>
        <point x="50" y="0.98"/>
        <point x="65" y="1.00"/>
        <point x="80" y="1.00"/>
        <point x="90" y="0.95"/>
        <point x="100" y="0.88"/>
    </yield>

    <market>
        <point x="0" y="0.92"/>
        <point x="25" y="0.95"/>
        <point x="50" y="0.98"/>
        <point x="65" y="1.00"/>
        <point x="80" y="1.00"/>
        <point x="90" y="0.95"/>
        <point x="100" y="0.88"/>
    </market>

    <fertility>
        <point x="1.0" y="0.20"/>
        <point x="2.0" y="0.65"/>
        <point x="2.5" y="1.00"/>
        <point x="3.25" y="1.00"/>
        <point x="4.0" y="0.75"/>
        <point x="5.0" y="0.35"/>
    </fertility>

    <lossTrend>
        <point x="0.00" y="1.00"/>
        <point x="0.03" y="1.00"/>
        <point x="0.10" y="0.75"/>
    </lossTrend>

    <feedEnergy>
        <fillType name="FORAGE" energy="1.00"/>
        <fillType name="FORAGEPLUS" energy="1.00"/>
        <fillType name="ROUNDBALE" energy="1.00"/>
        <fillType name="SILAGE" energy="0.82"/>
        <fillType name="DRYGRASS_WINDROW" energy="0.70"/>
        <fillType name="GRASS_WINDROW" energy="0.55"/>
        <fillType name="CLOVER_WINDROW" energy="0.55"/>
        <fillType name="SUGARBEET_CUT" energy="0.85"/>
        <fillType name="CALFMILK" energy="1.20"/>
    </feedEnergy>

    <!--
        Sheep (S5, 2026-10-13): candidates for the offline calibration, off until the weight
        model's SHEEP type is enabled. One BCS point is 10-13% of a ewe's live weight (FAS; AHDB
        Table 4 uses about 10%): with BCS = 1 + 4r, reserveCapacityRatio 0.48 is 12% of the
        reference weight a point (cattle 0.20: 5%). Ewes gain a BCS point in 6-8 weeks on good
        grass (AHDB) - the energy they eat sets that; gainRatePerMonth 0.30 (1.2 BCS) only caps a
        lamb finished on concentrate - and lose condition fast in early lactation. The
        reference ewe is at BCS 3 (referenceReserve 0.5). Fat classes: EUROP 1 / 2 / 3L / 3H / 4L /
        4H / 5 on the reserve (a model mapping, not tissue fat); the finish score is the reserve x
        100, so yield and market read the classes directly, one value a class (a 0.1 ramp at each
        boundary): 1.00 at classes 2-3L (the target R3L), 0.99 at 3H,
        0.97 at 4L, 0.955 at 4H (AHDB: R4H about 30p/kg below R3L), 0.90 at 5, 0.95 at 1 (the ends
        extrapolated). Over-fat lambs lose saleable meat, not price per kg. finishingTarget: x =
        weight / slaughter reference; a ram lamb on full feed is mid-3L at the reference (0.45) and
        enters 4L at 1.15 (AHDB: lambs reach 3L at about half their mature weight); wethers read it
        at x + 0.09, ewe lambs at x + 0.18 (a ram can be 7.7 kg heavier at the same fatness). BCS
        does not change conception in this batch (S7). A newborn lamb is lean (birthReserve 0.25,
        fat class 2: newborn lambs hold a few percent body fat) and lays fat down as it is finished.
        A lamb at its market weight is ready from fat class 2 and over-fat from 4L
        (finishReadyReserve, finishOverfatReserve: the design's sellable 2-3H). Lambs are the
        never-lambed under 12 months (youngUntilMonth, continuous age); a maiden ewe from 12 months
        is a ewe hogg: BCS shown, no finishing target, her reserve capped at heiferReserveCeiling
        0.55 (BCS 3.2; a model cap, not a mating or flushing target). A ewe that has lambed is
        capped at cowReserveCeiling 0.80 (BCS 4.2). Sheep are covered only under the weight
        model v2 with the real-equivalent nutrition model, from birth (calfLegacyUntilAgeMonth 0:
        a lamb finished at 3-5 months shows its fat class and its meat follows it).
    -->
    <species name="SHEEP" calfLegacyUntilAgeMonth="0" initialReserve="0.50" reserveCapacityRatio="0.48" referenceReserve="0.50" minReserve="0.05"
        gainRatePerMonth="0.30" lossRatePerMonth="0.25" heiferReserveCeiling="0.55" cowReserveCeiling="0.80"
        steerFinishShift="0.09" femaleFinishShift="0.18" youngUntilMonth="12" fertilityEffect="0" birthReserve="0.25"
        finishReadyReserve="0.25" finishOverfatReserve="0.58">
        <finishingTarget>
            <point x="0.50" y="0.30"/>
            <point x="0.80" y="0.38"/>
            <point x="1.00" y="0.45"/>
            <point x="1.15" y="0.58"/>
            <point x="1.30" y="0.70"/>
            <point x="1.50" y="0.82"/>
        </finishingTarget>
        <finishScore>
            <point x="0" y="0"/>
            <point x="1" y="100"/>
        </finishScore>
        <yield>
            <point x="24.9" y="0.95"/>
            <point x="25" y="1.00"/>
            <point x="49.9" y="1.00"/>
            <point x="50" y="0.99"/>
            <point x="57.9" y="0.99"/>
            <point x="58" y="0.97"/>
            <point x="65.9" y="0.97"/>
            <point x="66" y="0.955"/>
            <point x="74.9" y="0.955"/>
            <point x="75" y="0.90"/>
        </yield>
        <market>
            <point x="24.9" y="0.95"/>
            <point x="25" y="1.00"/>
            <point x="49.9" y="1.00"/>
            <point x="50" y="0.99"/>
            <point x="57.9" y="0.99"/>
            <point x="58" y="0.97"/>
            <point x="65.9" y="0.97"/>
            <point x="66" y="0.955"/>
            <point x="74.9" y="0.955"/>
            <point x="75" y="0.90"/>
        </market>
        <fatClass name="1" minReserve="0"/>
        <fatClass name="2" minReserve="0.25"/>
        <fatClass name="3L" minReserve="0.38"/>
        <fatClass name="3H" minReserve="0.50"/>
        <fatClass name="4L" minReserve="0.58"/>
        <fatClass name="4H" minReserve="0.66"/>
        <fatClass name="5" minReserve="0.75"/>
    </species>
</bodyCondition>
